WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.273 --> 00:00:04.893
- On this episode, Lewis
stops by to talk greatness.

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:08.863
(hip-hop music)

00:00:17.453 --> 00:00:22.453
You ask questions and I answer them.

00:00:23.153 --> 00:00:25.092
This is

00:00:25.092 --> 00:00:28.655
The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:31.895 --> 00:00:34.170
Hey everybody,
this is Gary Vay-ner-chuk

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.772
and this is episode 157

00:00:35.772 --> 00:00:38.930
of The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:41.437
A.K.A, if you've got a book

00:00:41.437 --> 00:00:44.212
come on and hawk that shit on the show.

00:00:44.212 --> 00:00:46.278
First my father-in-law,
now my good buddy, Lewis.

00:00:46.278 --> 00:00:48.751
Lewis, for the very few
in the Vayner Nation

00:00:48.751 --> 00:00:52.039
that don't crossover and
know who you already are,

00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:54.497
why don't you give a
little bit of your spiel.

00:00:54.497 --> 00:00:57.028
And then India and whatever
will get into the show.

00:00:57.028 --> 00:00:59.675
- Okay, a little bit about me?

00:00:59.675 --> 00:01:00.197
- Yes.

00:01:00.197 --> 00:01:02.331
- About me, I met this guy,

00:01:02.331 --> 00:01:04.675
how many years ago did I meet
you, maybe seven years ago?

00:01:04.675 --> 00:01:05.942
- Was it the during the Crush It! tour?

00:01:05.942 --> 00:01:07.395
- 2009, 2008?

00:01:07.395 --> 00:01:09.085
- [Both] 2009.

00:01:09.085 --> 00:01:11.331
- This guy inspired me to build a business

00:01:11.331 --> 00:01:12.550
and take it to a whole 'nother level.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:13.548
I was in a transition.

00:01:13.548 --> 00:01:15.033
I was sleeping on my sister's couch.

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:16.532
I had a cast on, I broke my wrist

00:01:16.532 --> 00:01:18.214
playing professional football.

00:01:18.214 --> 00:01:21.755
And the amount of hustle that this guy had

00:01:21.755 --> 00:01:23.532
really inspired me to

00:01:23.532 --> 00:01:25.354
figure out what I wanted
to do next with my life.

00:01:25.354 --> 00:01:29.032
So I spent a lot of time
researching, finding mentors,

00:01:29.032 --> 00:01:32.807
studying, taking a lot of
action, making a ton of mistakes.

00:01:32.807 --> 00:01:35.414
But learning along the way
in the process of taking

00:01:35.414 --> 00:01:38.440
that action and built a
couple seven-figure businesses

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.887
along the way and then started a podcast

00:01:40.887 --> 00:01:42.571
and that's where the book is coming from.

00:01:42.571 --> 00:01:43.987
- The podcast seems to be the place

00:01:43.987 --> 00:01:46.065
where your brand took a
different level, right?

00:01:46.065 --> 00:01:47.969
Like for me, from afar,
- [Lewis] Next level.

00:01:47.969 --> 00:01:49.512
- [Gary] you know, you
were playing the space

00:01:49.512 --> 00:01:51.486
and a lot of people try
to build personal brands

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:52.287
and things of that nature

00:01:52.287 --> 00:01:53.866
and how does one differentiate?

00:01:53.866 --> 00:01:55.770
When did you get serious
about the podcast?

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.592
Why did you get serious about the podcast?

00:01:57.592 --> 00:01:59.032
- It's interesting because you mentioned

00:01:59.032 --> 00:02:01.029
something to me in the car
in St. Louis, Missouri,

00:02:01.029 --> 00:02:03.153
when I drove you from an
event to another event

00:02:03.153 --> 00:02:04.878
or something in a hotel,
- [Gary] I remember.

00:02:04.878 --> 00:02:07.889
- [Lewis] and you said, "I
see you making a lot of money

00:02:07.889 --> 00:02:10.560
"and building an audience, but you're not

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.883
"at the mainstream level yet."

00:02:11.883 --> 00:02:14.241
You said something like, "You're
not at the mainstream media

00:02:14.241 --> 00:02:16.150
"level yet, and that's
where I wanna see you go."

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.491
So you said this to me and I
remember taking it to heart

00:02:18.491 --> 00:02:20.983
because I was making a lot
of money, I was growing.

00:02:20.983 --> 00:02:23.005
But I was like, "Do people in the press

00:02:23.005 --> 00:02:24.328
"wanna have me on their show?"

00:02:24.328 --> 00:02:25.048
- Yup.

00:02:25.048 --> 00:02:26.649
- And I said I gotta build--

00:02:26.649 --> 00:02:27.822
- You've made the main-stream!

00:02:27.822 --> 00:02:29.181
- (laughs) Right, exactly.

00:02:29.181 --> 00:02:31.328
And so I spent a lot of
time really figuring out

00:02:31.328 --> 00:02:32.663
what does that mean to build a brand,

00:02:32.663 --> 00:02:35.682
what does that mean to create
different type of content?

00:02:35.682 --> 00:02:39.106
The podcasting world in 2012, late 2012

00:02:39.106 --> 00:02:41.626
when I was seeing other
friends getting into it,

00:02:41.626 --> 00:02:43.587
I realized this is
something that I could--

00:02:43.587 --> 00:02:44.284
- This is happening.

00:02:44.284 --> 00:02:45.503
- This is happening,

00:02:45.503 --> 00:02:48.678
and I was like I think I can do this.

00:02:48.678 --> 00:02:49.798
If these guys can do it, I could do it.

00:02:49.798 --> 00:02:50.448
- Yup.

00:02:50.448 --> 00:02:51.598
- I didn't know what to expect.

00:02:51.598 --> 00:02:52.724
I just said for the first year

00:02:52.724 --> 00:02:54.662
I'm not gonna try to make any money.

00:02:54.662 --> 00:02:56.186
I'm not gonna take any sponsors.

00:02:56.186 --> 00:02:57.541
I'm just gonna go--

00:02:57.541 --> 00:02:58.806
- And interview people, put out content.

00:02:58.806 --> 00:03:00.618
- And interview the
best people I could find

00:03:00.618 --> 00:03:02.022
and give them my all.

00:03:02.022 --> 00:03:03.680
- And so it's interesting, in March,

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.087
I come out with The #AskGaryVee book.

00:03:05.087 --> 00:03:07.862
That's the play of your
podcast into the book.

00:03:07.862 --> 00:03:09.487
What are you promising people

00:03:09.487 --> 00:03:12.262
that have listened to
your podcast, in this book

00:03:12.262 --> 00:03:14.064
that doesn't translate from the--

00:03:14.064 --> 00:03:16.081
- Here's the thing, I interview
all these inspiring people,

00:03:16.081 --> 00:03:17.699
you've been on the show
and it's like we get

00:03:17.699 --> 00:03:20.852
these great lessons, we hear
these stories and that's great.

00:03:20.852 --> 00:03:24.627
But what are the actionable
exercises and practices

00:03:24.627 --> 00:03:26.683
that we can use in our daily life

00:03:26.683 --> 00:03:28.723
to take that action and get the results.

00:03:28.723 --> 00:03:29.942
It's one thing to be inspired--

00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:30.836
- Did you write this?

00:03:30.836 --> 00:03:31.718
Did you do it the way I do

00:03:31.718 --> 00:03:33.762
where you have a ghostwriter
and you audio it?

00:03:33.762 --> 00:03:34.732
- I work with a couple
people who have helped me

00:03:34.732 --> 00:03:36.362
but it's a lot of
interviewing back and forth,

00:03:36.362 --> 00:03:37.797
a lot of editing back and forth.

00:03:37.797 --> 00:03:39.524
- Yup, can you write?

00:03:39.524 --> 00:03:40.483
- A little bit.

00:03:40.483 --> 00:03:43.173
As a dyslexic guy, it's kinda challenging.

00:03:43.173 --> 00:03:44.801
You know, it's a hard work for me.

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:47.798
- I can't write, right
India and Steve? (laughs)

00:03:47.798 --> 00:03:49.377
- That's why one of the principles--

00:03:49.377 --> 00:03:50.860
- Have you ever gotten an email from me?

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.440
(laughs)

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.099
- One of the principles is
building a championship team.

00:03:55.099 --> 00:03:57.362
And I know that in order
to put out a great product

00:03:57.362 --> 00:03:59.336
I need to have a great team around me

00:03:59.336 --> 00:04:02.459
of writers, editors, publisher,
to support the process.

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:03.712
Because I couldn't do it on my own.

00:04:03.712 --> 00:04:05.002
- Very cool man.
- [Lewis] There's no way.

00:04:05.002 --> 00:04:06.135
- [Gary] Good for you,
congrats, I'm proud of you.

00:04:06.135 --> 00:04:06.952
When's it out?

00:04:06.952 --> 00:04:07.857
- It's out today.

00:04:07.857 --> 00:04:11.086
- Alright, well then let's
link that up, it's a big day.

00:04:11.086 --> 00:04:12.155
I'm humbled that you want to be

00:04:12.155 --> 00:04:13.557
on this show on your big day.

00:04:13.557 --> 00:04:15.078
What else are you doing today media-wise?

00:04:15.078 --> 00:04:16.598
- I did Fox and Friends this morning,

00:04:16.598 --> 00:04:19.262
or I did Good Day New
York this morning, Fox 5,

00:04:19.262 --> 00:04:21.010
I'm doing Fox and Friends this weekend.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:24.179
But this is the most important
show of the whole week.

00:04:24.179 --> 00:04:28.256
- On that note India, let's get into this

00:04:28.256 --> 00:04:30.773
- [Both] show.

00:04:30.773 --> 00:04:32.016
- [Gary] I like when we get that thing.

00:04:32.016 --> 00:04:33.276
What is that called, again?

00:04:33.276 --> 00:04:34.137
- [DRock] Harmony.
- [India] I don't know.

00:04:34.137 --> 00:04:34.644
- [Gary] What's that?

00:04:34.644 --> 00:04:35.444
- [DRock] Harmony.
- [India] It's not.

00:04:35.444 --> 00:04:36.019
- [Gary] Harmony.

00:04:36.019 --> 00:04:37.309
- [India] I don't think that's what it is.

00:04:37.309 --> 00:04:40.110
- It's Harmony, we're harmonizing.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:41.488
Alright, let's go India.

00:04:41.488 --> 00:04:43.638
- [Voiceover] Martina asks, "When making

00:04:43.638 --> 00:04:45.931
"important decisions do
you trust your own gut

00:04:45.931 --> 00:04:49.651
"and experience or do you
count on some advisor as well?"

00:04:49.651 --> 00:04:52.192
- Who's your go-to person
on a real decision?

00:04:52.192 --> 00:04:53.469
Do you have one?

00:04:53.469 --> 00:04:55.058
- You know, it used to be my Dad,

00:04:55.058 --> 00:04:59.227
but he had a pretty extreme car
accident about 10 years ago,

00:04:59.227 --> 00:05:01.316
so he can't really
comprehend certain things

00:05:01.316 --> 00:05:02.454
at that level anymore.

00:05:02.454 --> 00:05:04.860
But I go to a guy named Stewart Jenkins.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.110
Who works at a big brand called Deckers,

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.837
a billion dollar shoe company

00:05:08.837 --> 00:05:12.204
that owns Ugg and Timba,
and some of those brands.

00:05:12.204 --> 00:05:15.034
And he's just like a straight
shooter, tells me how it is,

00:05:15.034 --> 00:05:16.033
great family guy.

00:05:16.033 --> 00:05:17.893
- Doesn't try to pander to what

00:05:17.893 --> 00:05:19.503
you want to hear?
- [Lewis] He doesn't deal with my ego

00:05:19.503 --> 00:05:20.728
he just tells me.

00:05:20.728 --> 00:05:22.512
He's got such good integrity and morals.

00:05:22.512 --> 00:05:23.989
- That's why I don't speak to anybody,

00:05:23.989 --> 00:05:25.375
I don't want anybody touching my ego.

00:05:25.375 --> 00:05:27.198
- (laughs) Exactly.

00:05:27.198 --> 00:05:29.938
So I talk to him with real
issues that speak to the heart.

00:05:29.938 --> 00:05:33.205
- What percentage of decisions
do you make on your gut?

00:05:33.205 --> 00:05:33.944
- A lot.

00:05:33.944 --> 00:05:35.760
- Like what percentage?

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.212
- Probably 100%.

00:05:37.212 --> 00:05:40.405
- OK, so you really don't
give a shit what Stewart says.

00:05:40.405 --> 00:05:42.750
- No, I advise with
everyone on tough decisions

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.440
but I always know the answer.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:45.245
- Got it.

00:05:45.245 --> 00:05:46.762
- You know what I mean,
it's like I know the answer,

00:05:46.762 --> 00:05:47.793
but I feel like I have to make sure.
- [Gary] Has anybody

00:05:47.793 --> 00:05:49.452
wavered you from the answer?

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:51.830
- People have given me different opinions

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.627
but I always know, yes, they have.

00:05:53.627 --> 00:05:55.928
They have, they waver
me, and then I realize

00:05:55.928 --> 00:05:57.645
I shouldn't have taken that advice.

00:05:57.645 --> 00:05:59.907
- So at times, where you
haven't gone with your gut

00:05:59.907 --> 00:06:01.532
and you've gone with people
that you think have made it

00:06:01.532 --> 00:06:03.309
or other things, it hasn't paid off,

00:06:03.309 --> 00:06:05.423
so you're now defaulting
as you're maturing

00:06:05.423 --> 00:06:06.629
into your own place.
- [Lewis] Yes.

00:06:06.629 --> 00:06:09.771
- [Gary] I mean for me,
I've always been that way.

00:06:09.771 --> 00:06:11.861
And I think that what happened,
it's interesting to hear

00:06:11.861 --> 00:06:13.385
your perspective, it's fun
to have guests a little bit

00:06:13.385 --> 00:06:15.099
because you would've gotten my answer of

00:06:15.099 --> 00:06:16.770
you know, no!

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.970
I respect my Mom and Dad
and things of that nature,

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:20.108
but I don't even,

00:06:20.108 --> 00:06:21.187
I love them with all of my heart,

00:06:21.187 --> 00:06:22.487
I just make my own decisions.

00:06:22.487 --> 00:06:25.262
I don't know what else to say about this.

00:06:25.262 --> 00:06:26.888
But it's interesting that--

00:06:26.888 --> 00:06:28.384
- I come from an athlete background,

00:06:28.384 --> 00:06:30.961
so I was constantly coached,
constantly getting feedback.

00:06:30.961 --> 00:06:32.540
So I like to have the coaching,

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:33.306
- That makes sense.

00:06:33.306 --> 00:06:34.827
- in an environment so
I can just take action

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:36.633
and apply the information.

00:06:36.633 --> 00:06:38.632
But a lot of the times, I know the answer,

00:06:38.632 --> 00:06:40.644
it's just trusting
myself a hundred percent.

00:06:40.644 --> 00:06:41.486
- That makes a lot of sense.

00:06:41.486 --> 00:06:44.288
I think the one thing that is
an actual item of this show

00:06:44.288 --> 00:06:45.287
taking the theme from your book

00:06:45.287 --> 00:06:48.036
and we'll continue to act on it.

00:06:48.036 --> 00:06:50.824
I think you should really try,
if you're watching the show,

00:06:50.824 --> 00:06:54.644
to try the opposite on the
next decent sized decision.

00:06:54.644 --> 00:06:56.411
As long as it's not a top
three decision in your life,

00:06:56.411 --> 00:06:57.601
then do you.

00:06:57.601 --> 00:07:00.084
If it's a fourth biggest
decision type of thing,

00:07:00.084 --> 00:07:02.041
try the other way as context.

00:07:02.041 --> 00:07:04.001
I may even try this, no I can't.

00:07:04.001 --> 00:07:05.103
But (laughs)

00:07:05.103 --> 00:07:07.158
that didn't even last a sentence.

00:07:07.158 --> 00:07:09.224
But, I think that would be interesting.

00:07:09.224 --> 00:07:11.906
Because I'm intrigued by
you ending up in this place.

00:07:11.906 --> 00:07:14.437
My intuition is a lot of
people end up in that place

00:07:14.437 --> 00:07:16.470
as they get older and older,
because at some level,

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:20.996
you'd rather, for me, I'd
rather go down my way.

00:07:20.996 --> 00:07:22.575
That to me is probably the thing.

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:23.944
When you're making real decisions

00:07:23.944 --> 00:07:25.373
there's upside and there's downside.

00:07:25.373 --> 00:07:26.686
That's why they're big decisions.

00:07:26.686 --> 00:07:28.152
If I'm gonna go down,

00:07:28.152 --> 00:07:30.507
I wanna go down because I screwed up.

00:07:30.507 --> 00:07:32.292
Not because somebody else gave me advice

00:07:32.292 --> 00:07:33.337
and it just didn't work out.

00:07:33.337 --> 00:07:35.158
That's just, yeah, alright India.

00:07:35.158 --> 00:07:36.747
- I think that segues really
well into the next question.

00:07:36.747 --> 00:07:38.973
- [Gary] Oh, a lot of segueing going on.

00:07:40.453 --> 00:07:42.797
- [Voiceover] Gabriel asks,
"What is the best example

00:07:42.797 --> 00:07:45.560
"in your life, where your ego
has gotten the best of you?

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.799
"How significant was it in retrospect?"

00:07:48.799 --> 00:07:49.554
- Well, this is great, right,

00:07:49.554 --> 00:07:51.180
because you've already dropped ego.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.566
I almost called this The Ego Show.

00:07:53.566 --> 00:07:57.185
So you know, what was the question?

00:07:58.075 --> 00:07:59.329
- When the ego got the best of you?

00:07:59.329 --> 00:08:01.512
- [India] And how significant
was it in retrospect?

00:08:01.512 --> 00:08:02.591
- Oh my gosh, should I go?

00:08:02.591 --> 00:08:03.752
- Sure. Yeah go ahead.

00:08:03.752 --> 00:08:05.192
- For me, oh man.

00:08:05.192 --> 00:08:07.568
Any relationship that I've been in

00:08:07.568 --> 00:08:10.305
I allow my ego to get the
best of me in the past.

00:08:10.305 --> 00:08:11.687
Where it's like--
- With, like, girls you mean?

00:08:11.687 --> 00:08:12.913
- Yes, yes.
- [Gary] Okay, go ahead.

00:08:12.913 --> 00:08:14.910
- [Lewis] And then it just
affects me, it like consumes

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:16.026
every decision.
- [Gary] What's the ego part?

00:08:16.026 --> 00:08:17.567
Like you think you're hotter than them?

00:08:17.567 --> 00:08:22.428
- No, no, it's like after we break up

00:08:22.428 --> 00:08:23.916
or end things, it's like--

00:08:23.916 --> 00:08:25.634
- You don't wanna call first?

00:08:25.634 --> 00:08:28.027
And say, "That was a cool run"?

00:08:28.027 --> 00:08:30.128
- I don't know it's just like
I'm still holding onto things

00:08:30.128 --> 00:08:32.826
and I can't let certain
things go, and I'm mad still

00:08:32.826 --> 00:08:34.586
or pissed, or why it didn't work out.

00:08:34.586 --> 00:08:36.431
And that, holding onto things

00:08:36.431 --> 00:08:37.534
as opposed to fully letting go,

00:08:37.534 --> 00:08:38.312
- Yeah.

00:08:38.312 --> 00:08:40.727
- will hold me back in
other areas of my life,

00:08:40.727 --> 00:08:42.654
and just my health, my business.

00:08:42.654 --> 00:08:44.186
Like making clear decisions.

00:08:44.186 --> 00:08:46.481
And that ego, I gotta fully
let go in those situations.

00:08:46.481 --> 00:08:47.251
- Interesting.

00:08:47.251 --> 00:08:47.866
- What about you?

00:08:47.866 --> 00:08:50.664
- For me, I think with ego,

00:08:50.664 --> 00:08:53.706
I think it's things that I don't know.

00:08:53.706 --> 00:08:54.530
- I thought you know everything.

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:57.131
- Well, I know everything
that actually has happened.

00:08:57.131 --> 00:09:00.405
So, maybe my ego of recognizing,

00:09:00.405 --> 00:09:02.227
well no, hear me out India.

00:09:02.227 --> 00:09:03.329
Like, let me give you an example.

00:09:03.329 --> 00:09:06.796
I know you're laughing over
there and having a good time.

00:09:06.796 --> 00:09:07.706
Let me explain.

00:09:07.706 --> 00:09:09.280
I would tell you that I

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:13.374
slash, it was the right business
decision, but ego of not

00:09:13.374 --> 00:09:16.166
doing a lot of television
shows because I feel like

00:09:16.166 --> 00:09:19.152
the world's gonna change
