00:13:06.329 --> 00:13:07.687
DRock's shaking his head, right?

00:13:07.687 --> 00:13:09.429
- What about with intimate
or personal relationships,

00:13:09.429 --> 00:13:10.949
with friends or family?

00:13:10.949 --> 00:13:13.631
Now do you just say screw you,
I'm off to the next person?

00:13:13.631 --> 00:13:15.745
- I haven't really lost
anybody who I would call

00:13:15.745 --> 00:13:17.067
in my most inner circle.

00:13:17.067 --> 00:13:20.679
I've had relationships
that have, like, my longest

00:13:20.679 --> 00:13:25.152
girlfriend relationships
that ended were predicated

00:13:25.152 --> 00:13:27.341
on me sitting on it longer than,

00:13:27.341 --> 00:13:29.740
it was in my head for a long time anyway.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.683
So, yeah, I mean, I don't know.

00:13:32.853 --> 00:13:35.710
I've been very blessed that
somebody that meant the world

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:38.288
to me hasn't decided to leave me.

00:13:38.288 --> 00:13:40.397
So that's one part of it.

00:13:40.397 --> 00:13:41.547
And, the other part is,

00:13:41.547 --> 00:13:44.407
I haven't, outside of a couple long-term

00:13:44.407 --> 00:13:47.111
girlfriend relationships, I
haven't parted from anything

00:13:47.111 --> 00:13:49.220
that's been in my inner core.

00:13:50.211 --> 00:13:54.389
In business, I've had to fire
people I care a lot about.

00:13:54.389 --> 00:13:57.489
But, I've come to a
place where I recognize

00:13:57.489 --> 00:13:59.487
that I was doing more
harm by keeping them here

00:13:59.487 --> 00:14:02.529
and giving them no growth
here, Wine Library.

00:14:02.529 --> 00:14:05.489
So, yeah, that's that.

00:14:05.489 --> 00:14:06.382
Anything that you want to add on that?

00:14:06.382 --> 00:14:07.148
- No.

00:14:07.148 --> 00:14:07.949
- Alright. Let's do it.

00:14:07.949 --> 00:14:10.006
- This is from Jarek.

00:14:10.006 --> 00:14:12.070
- Oh, Jarek.

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:13.085
I've been seeing Jarek
- [Lewis] Is it Robbins?

00:14:13.085 --> 00:14:13.895
- [Gary] show up a bunch.

00:14:13.895 --> 00:14:16.889
Hamilton, he's been popping
up a bunch lately on my feed.

00:14:16.889 --> 00:14:18.166
- Hey what's going on Gary Vee?

00:14:18.166 --> 00:14:21.082
My company got accepted to be
a part of the Alpha Program

00:14:21.082 --> 00:14:23.982
at Collision 2016 in New Orleans.

00:14:23.982 --> 00:14:25.607
I've never been to an event like this,

00:14:25.607 --> 00:14:27.302
so I'm not really sure
what to prepare for,

00:14:27.302 --> 00:14:28.823
how to prepare for it.

00:14:28.823 --> 00:14:31.881
I was wondering, what would
you do if you were in my shoes,

00:14:31.881 --> 00:14:34.433
if you were going to not
necessarily look for money,

00:14:34.433 --> 00:14:36.587
but more looking to make contacts?

00:14:36.587 --> 00:14:37.878
I'd appreciate any information,

00:14:37.878 --> 00:14:39.979
any insights you might
have on the situation.

00:14:39.979 --> 00:14:40.757
Thanks, Gary.
- [Gary] That's real nice.

00:14:40.757 --> 00:14:43.380
Jarek, really legit American
flag waving so beautifully

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:45.180
in the background.

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.805
I did notice the Giants t-shirt,

00:14:46.805 --> 00:14:48.802
so I'm a little bit pissed with Jarek now.

00:14:48.802 --> 00:14:49.858
Lewis any thoughts on that?

00:14:49.858 --> 00:14:52.261
I think you wanted to establish your name

00:14:52.261 --> 00:14:54.642
in the marketing and business world.

00:14:54.642 --> 00:14:57.086
I assume you started
going to a lot of events

00:14:57.086 --> 00:14:59.519
for the first time through
the last half decade?

00:14:59.519 --> 00:15:00.318
How did you approach it?

00:15:00.318 --> 00:15:03.324
- I remember when I was
broke, on my sister's couch,

00:15:03.324 --> 00:15:06.573
I took a Greyhound to one
of my first events in 2008.

00:15:06.573 --> 00:15:07.583
It was in New York.

00:15:07.583 --> 00:15:11.184
It was like a sports
film festival in Philly.

00:15:11.184 --> 00:15:14.764
I took a Greyhound,
and I had a suit jacket

00:15:14.764 --> 00:15:16.744
and a carry-on case.

00:15:16.744 --> 00:15:21.064
I remember I had a hostel
room that I got for like $17.

00:15:21.064 --> 00:15:22.340
Because I knew the value.

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:24.024
I met a guy named Ben
Sterner, who I think you know

00:15:24.024 --> 00:15:25.277
who works out of here.

00:15:25.277 --> 00:15:26.577
He was like, "You've got
to come to this event.

00:15:26.577 --> 00:15:27.622
"You're gonna meet a
lot of powerful people

00:15:27.622 --> 00:15:28.655
"that's gonna help your business,

00:15:28.655 --> 00:15:30.757
"your relationships, your brand."

00:15:30.757 --> 00:15:32.463
So I was like I gotta get there.

00:15:32.463 --> 00:15:34.622
I rented a hostel for $17 the first night.

00:15:34.622 --> 00:15:36.654
I walk in, late at night

00:15:36.654 --> 00:15:38.662
and there's throw-up all over the ground.

00:15:38.662 --> 00:15:41.204
There's throw-up all over the
floor, all over the bathroom,

00:15:41.204 --> 00:15:44.398
and 20 snoring, European guys in the room.

00:15:44.398 --> 00:15:46.893
But I knew the value right
when I got to this event

00:15:46.893 --> 00:15:48.680
how important it was to connect with

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:51.362
influencers and powerful individuals.

00:15:51.362 --> 00:15:54.056
And I stayed up all night with people

00:15:54.056 --> 00:15:55.577
and just built a relationship with them.

00:15:55.577 --> 00:15:56.692
It wasn't about being at the event

00:15:56.692 --> 00:15:58.978
it was figuring out where people
were going after the event.

00:15:58.978 --> 00:16:00.081
And getting in with their friends

00:16:00.081 --> 00:16:02.275
and then creating friendships with people.

00:16:02.275 --> 00:16:06.176
I never talked about
business or asked for advice.

00:16:06.176 --> 00:16:08.218
I just said, "What's
going on in your life?"

00:16:08.218 --> 00:16:10.135
Like how can we be buddy-buddy?

00:16:10.135 --> 00:16:12.882
And, let's do thumb-wrestling
wars, whatever,

00:16:12.882 --> 00:16:14.580
like I did with you in 2009 or something.

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:17.169
It's like let's have fun
as opposed to talking about

00:16:17.169 --> 00:16:18.899
the thing you don't want
to talk about right now.

00:16:18.899 --> 00:16:21.939
- You know to me, Jarek, I think
it's a really good question

00:16:21.939 --> 00:16:25.237
and I think that's right.

00:16:25.237 --> 00:16:28.697
I think at some level, if
you're not meeting people,

00:16:28.697 --> 00:16:30.508
you're not hitting on your KPI.

00:16:30.508 --> 00:16:32.876
I think Lewis speaks to
patience in a relationship,

00:16:32.876 --> 00:16:34.629
I think it really matters.

00:16:34.629 --> 00:16:36.417
Trying to throw around your business card

00:16:36.417 --> 00:16:40.725
or pitch your business, is
completely the opposite move.

00:16:40.725 --> 00:16:42.593
- Not to cut you off,
but to set an example,

00:16:42.593 --> 00:16:46.377
I've been essentially jabbing
for six years with you.

00:16:46.377 --> 00:16:47.074
- That's right.

00:16:47.074 --> 00:16:47.829
- I never asked for anything.

00:16:47.829 --> 00:16:49.923
- I bought a shit-load of these books.

00:16:49.923 --> 00:16:51.055
- But I've never asked for anything

00:16:51.055 --> 00:16:52.217
except for
- [Gary] This book.

00:16:52.217 --> 00:16:53.449
- [Lewis] The blurb on the back.

00:16:53.449 --> 00:16:54.236
Which you know.

00:16:54.236 --> 00:16:54.794
- Which is a big deal

00:16:54.794 --> 00:16:55.932
because I don't like giving them out.

00:16:55.932 --> 00:16:58.435
- And also hey, can I come on the show.

00:16:58.435 --> 00:17:00.134
And, you bought some books, yes.

00:17:00.134 --> 00:17:01.376
- Which is my own advice, right?

00:17:01.376 --> 00:17:04.576
I talk about that a lot which
is cash in all those chips

00:17:04.576 --> 00:17:05.707
when you've got your signature moment.

00:17:05.707 --> 00:17:07.952
Like you need this to do well.

00:17:07.952 --> 00:17:10.175
It's another watershed
moment in your career.

00:17:10.175 --> 00:17:11.489
- Before, what was that guy's name again?

00:17:11.489 --> 00:17:12.115
- Jarek.

00:17:12.115 --> 00:17:15.133
- For Jarek, I would not
ask anyone for support

00:17:15.133 --> 00:17:16.410
or help right now.

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:19.003
I would just say how can I give
that person a relationship.

00:17:19.003 --> 00:17:20.868
- Listening to Lewis' narrative, Jarek,

00:17:20.868 --> 00:17:22.659
I think one of the
biggest things that people

00:17:22.659 --> 00:17:24.801
make mistakes for, is
they go for short-term

00:17:24.801 --> 00:17:27.797
nickels and dimes instead
of long-term dollars.

00:17:27.797 --> 00:17:31.700
So, if you find, the more
important the person you encounter

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:35.031
in New Orleans, the more you
should not ask for anything.

00:17:35.031 --> 00:17:37.028
Too many times, the amount
of people that roll up on me,

00:17:37.028 --> 00:17:40.920
I have no idea who they are,
and they want $100,000 from me

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:43.333
because they're gonna
help me buy the Jets.

00:17:45.749 --> 00:17:48.032
It's pretty intense, there's no context.

00:17:48.032 --> 00:17:49.522
It's not the right move.

00:17:50.672 --> 00:17:51.992
Again, back to his story.

00:17:51.992 --> 00:17:55.905
Go sleep with 20 snoring
throw-up dudes for $17

00:17:55.905 --> 00:17:58.418
if you've got a practical
financial problem.

00:17:58.418 --> 00:18:01.416
Have the humility to go
sleep on your sister's couch,

00:18:01.416 --> 00:18:03.021
if you have a financial problem.

00:18:03.021 --> 00:18:04.762
If your financial problem
needs to be solved

00:18:04.762 --> 00:18:06.398
by you going to the most important people

00:18:06.398 --> 00:18:09.316
on first impact and asking for $25,000,

00:18:09.316 --> 00:18:11.042
you've put yourself in a bad position.

00:18:11.042 --> 00:18:13.340
So I think what you need to do
is put yourself in a position

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:14.943
where you put no pressure on yourself

00:18:14.943 --> 00:18:18.956
to close at this event,
but build at this event.

00:18:18.956 --> 00:18:19.947
- And look at the long-term.

00:18:19.947 --> 00:18:21.746
Think of relationships for
five years down the line.

00:18:21.746 --> 00:18:23.023
- Hundred percent, India?

00:18:23.023 --> 00:18:25.078
- Last one from Adam.

00:18:25.078 --> 00:18:26.927
Will privacy related, sorry--

00:18:26.927 --> 00:18:27.760
- Am I freaking you out?

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:29.407
- No, I'm kinda used to it.

00:18:29.407 --> 00:18:30.443
Will privacy related--

00:18:30.443 --> 00:18:33.487
- Well you said privacy like (laughs)

00:18:33.487 --> 00:18:36.359
- [Voiceover] Adam asks,
"Will privacy related issues

00:18:36.359 --> 00:18:38.480
"be the ultimate Achilles
heel for Social Networks

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:40.998
"including Facebook?"

00:18:40.998 --> 00:18:42.240
- I'll jump in here.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:44.492
The answer is no, because
there's a pendulum swing.

00:18:44.492 --> 00:18:47.418
The reason Snapchat
grew was because it was

00:18:47.418 --> 00:18:50.766
a safer place, a place
that stuff would disappear.

00:18:50.766 --> 00:18:53.427
I actually think the
next wave is gonna be,

00:18:53.427 --> 00:18:56.758
I think they'll be more
closed over the next decade.

00:18:56.758 --> 00:18:59.102
You've got Cyber Dust, Cube's thing,

00:18:59.102 --> 00:18:59.684
you've got Snapchat.

00:18:59.684 --> 00:19:01.019
I think there's more room for that.

00:19:01.019 --> 00:19:02.526
I think there will be another one or two.

00:19:02.526 --> 00:19:05.073
I think the reason WhatsApp and
other things of that nature,

00:19:05.073 --> 00:19:07.843
messaging apps are doing
well is in theory to people,

00:19:07.843 --> 00:19:09.864
it's closed by comparison.

00:19:10.824 --> 00:19:13.204
But I think actually
in the long long-term,

00:19:13.204 --> 00:19:15.878
10, 12, you know 8, 10, 12 years from now

00:19:15.878 --> 00:19:17.888
there will be another push back to open.

00:19:17.888 --> 00:19:19.060
It's generational, right?

00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:21.439
It's like fashion.

00:19:21.439 --> 00:19:22.437
It goes through ebbs and flows.

00:19:22.437 --> 00:19:25.340
As a matter of fact, with
everybody being so kind of hipster

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:26.663
and the way everybody is now,

00:19:26.663 --> 00:19:28.764
the other day I was thinking,
"Crap, I wonder where

00:19:28.764 --> 00:19:31.524
"like when are we gonna
go conservative again?"

00:19:31.524 --> 00:19:34.292
Like when everybody got
the Friends haircut.

00:19:34.292 --> 00:19:36.269
I'm curious and I was
thinking about grunge,

00:19:36.269 --> 00:19:37.262
which was crazy weird.

00:19:37.262 --> 00:19:38.422
Anyway, it made me think about that

00:19:38.422 --> 00:19:39.700
and that's how I think
about Social Networks.

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:41.093
This is the mood of a generation.

00:19:41.093 --> 00:19:42.776
The 60's were different
than the 70s and the 80s

00:19:42.776 --> 00:19:44.274
and 90s and 2000s.

00:19:44.274 --> 00:19:46.433
I definitely think people
will freak out about privacy.

00:19:46.433 --> 00:19:50.632
I think Ashley Madison is
a proxy to other things.

00:19:50.632 --> 00:19:53.712
Just imagine now all of us,
if all of our information

00:19:53.712 --> 00:19:57.578
of every text, email and
engagement, and I mean texts

00:19:57.578 --> 00:20:00.278
and WhatsApps and whatever
else you're using to creep.

00:20:00.278 --> 00:20:01.101
Tinder.

00:20:01.101 --> 00:20:03.881
Imagine all those conversations plop out

00:20:03.881 --> 00:20:04.964
and they're just searchable.

00:20:04.964 --> 00:20:07.399
Literally Lewis, everything
you've said digitally.

00:20:07.399 --> 00:20:10.089
Yeah, and so I actually
think that happens.

00:20:10.089 --> 00:20:11.719
And then I think Nirvana happens

00:20:11.719 --> 00:20:14.097
because everybody realizes
how flawed everybody is.

00:20:14.097 --> 00:20:17.336
So, I've got a very weird,
positive point of view

00:20:17.336 --> 00:20:18.523
in where this all goes.

00:20:18.523 --> 00:20:20.053
I think there'll be
some, I do think privacy

00:20:20.053 --> 00:20:23.733
will contain people to
some different behaviors.

00:20:23.733 --> 00:20:26.252
But I actually think if you
play the chess moves out,

00:20:26.252 --> 00:20:28.544
everybody's laundry gets aired out,

00:20:28.544 --> 00:20:30.791
which changes society forever.

00:20:30.791 --> 00:20:32.816
And we roll in a completely different way

00:20:32.816 --> 00:20:35.741
and the things we accept as
norms, the way we think about

00:20:35.741 --> 00:20:37.014
